# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-politics-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2 has "same_man_magistrate" and "citizen_obeys" and "magistrate_differs_citizen"; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2.2 has "magistrate_differs_citizen"; they differ when only "the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same" and "the virtue of the magistrate is different from the virtue of the citizen" are true, which the second allows; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2.3 has "magistrate_differs_citizen" and "same_man_magistrate ∧ magistrate_differs_citizen → ¬same_man_citizen"
aristotle-politics-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2.2 and aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same" and "the virtue of the magistrate is different from the virtue of the citizen" are true and "a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate" and "the virtue of the good man is in general the same as the virtue of the citizen" are false. Is S2, "But if we allow, that the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate may be the same, and a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate, it follows that the virtue of the one cannot in general be the same as the virtue of the other, although it may be true of some particular citizen; for the virtue of the magistrate must be different from the virtue of the citizen.", true then?
aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2 has "same_man_magistrate ∧ citizen_obeys ∧ magistrate_differs_citizen → ¬same_man_citizen" and "citizen_virtue_both → O citizen_masters_both"; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2.2 has "same_man_magistrate ∧ magistrate_differs_citizen → ¬same_man_citizen" and "citizen_virtue_both → O citizen_masters_both"; they differ when only "the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same", "the virtue of the magistrate is different from the virtue of the citizen", "the virtue of the good man is in general the same as the virtue of the citizen", "to know how to command and to obey, and to do both well, is the virtue of an accomplished citizen", "it ought to be that the citizen is master of and takes part in both commanding and obeying" and "it ought to be that the citizen is master of and takes part in both commanding and obeying" are true, which the first allows; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r2.3 has "citizen_virtue_both → O citizen_masters_both"
